  "text": "Accent: A variety of a language or dialect characterized by particular pronunciation, intonation, and vocabulary.\n\nAffix: A morpheme attached to a root or stem to modify its meaning, such as prefixes and suffixes.\n\nAllophone: A variant form of a phoneme that does not contrast with other variants in meaning.\n\nAllophony: The process by which a phoneme can have different allophones in different phonological environments.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to another word or phrase previously mentioned in the text.\n\nApocope: The dropping or omission of the final syllable or sound of a word.\n\nArticulation: The process of forming speech sounds by using the articulators such as the tongue, lips, and teeth.\n\nAustroasiatic: A language family that includes languages spoken in parts of Southeast Asia and mainland Southeast Asia.\n\nBantu: A branch of the Niger\u2013Congo language family spoken in Central and Southern Africa.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two languages fluently.\n\nCognate: A word that has a common etymological origin in two or more languages.\n\nConsonant: A speech sound produced by obstructing airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster: A group of consonants that occur together in a word without intervening vowels.\n\nConsonant gradation: A phonological process where consonants change in a predictable pattern depending on their position in a word.\n\nConsonant harmony: A phonological process where consonants in a word are modified to be similar in some feature, such as place or manner of articulation.\n\nConsonant mutation: A change in the phonetic character of a consonant due to its phonological environment.\n\nConsonant phoneme: A distinct sound in a language that is characterized by the way the airstream is obstructed.\n\nCrystal: A term used to describe the study of language in use, particularly in spoken interaction.\n\nDichotomy: A division into two distinct parts or categories.\n\nDiglossia: A situation where two varieties of a language are used in different social contexts, often with one being considered more prestigious.\n\nDisfluency: A break in the flow of speech, such as repetitions, hesitations, or false starts.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that begins with one vowel quality and glides to another.\n\nDissimilation: A phonological process where sounds become more different from each other.\n\nDiphthongization: The process of a single vowel sound becoming a diphthong.\n\nDitransitive: A verb that takes two objects, typically a direct and an indirect object.\n\nDolgoch: A term referring to the epenthetic insertion of a vowel in a consonant cluster to make it easier to pronounce.\n\nDialect: A variety of a language that is characteristic of a particular group or region.\n\nDigraph: A combination of two letters that represent a single sound or phoneme.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that involves a smooth transition from one vowel to another.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.",
